## 9) Building a Node in JavaScript

```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(value) {
    this.value = value;
    this.next = null;
    this.prev = null;
  }
}
```

### Explanation

Each new node starts with:

* a value
* no next node yet
* no previous node yet

---

## 10) Building a Doubly Linked List Class

```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(value) {
    this.value = value;
    this.next = null;
    this.prev = null;
  }
}

class DoublyLinkedList {
  constructor() {
    this.head = null;
    this.tail = null;
    this.length = 0;
  }
}
```

### What this stores

* `head`: first node
* `tail`: last node
* `length`: number of nodes

---

## 11) Append to the End

### Method: `append(value)`

This adds a new node at the end.

```javascript
append(value) {
  const newNode = new Node(value);

  if (!this.head) {
    this.head = newNode;
    this.tail = newNode;
  } else {
    newNode.prev = this.tail;
    this.tail.next = newNode;
    this.tail = newNode;
  }

  this.length++;
}
```

### What happens

If the list is empty:

* new node becomes both head and tail

If the list is not empty:

* new node’s `prev` points to old tail
* old tail’s `next` points to new node
* tail becomes new node

---

## 12) Prepend to the Beginning

### Method: `prepend(value)`

This adds a node at the beginning.

```javascript
prepend(value) {
  const newNode = new Node(value);

  if (!this.head) {
    this.head = newNode;
    this.tail = newNode;
  } else {
    newNode.next = this.head;
    this.head.prev = newNode;
    this.head = newNode;
  }

  this.length++;
}
```

### What happens

If the list is empty:

* new node becomes both head and tail

If the list already has nodes:

* new node points forward to current head
* current head points backward to new node
* head becomes new node

---

## 13) Print Forward

```javascript
printForward() {
  let current = this.head;
  let values = [];

  while (current) {
    values.push(current.value);
    current = current.next;
  }

  console.log(values.join(" <-> "));
}
```

### Example

If the list contains:

```javascript
10, 20, 30
```

Output:

```javascript
10 <-> 20 <-> 30
```

---

## 14) Print Backward

```javascript
printBackward() {
  let current = this.tail;
  let values = [];

  while (current) {
    values.push(current.value);
    current = current.prev;
  }

  console.log(values.join(" <-> "));
}
```

### Example

If the list contains:

```javascript
10, 20, 30
```

Backward output:

```javascript
30 <-> 20 <-> 10
```

This shows the extra power of a doubly linked list.

---

## 15) Remove from the Beginning

### Method: `removeHead()`

```javascript
removeHead() {
  if (!this.head) return null;

  const removedValue = this.head.value;

  if (this.length === 1) {
    this.head = null;
    this.tail = null;
  } else {
    this.head = this.head.next;
    this.head.prev = null;
  }

  this.length--;
  return removedValue;
}
```

### What happens

If there is only one node:

* head and tail both become `null`

Otherwise:

* move head to the next node
* clear the new head’s `prev`

---

## 16) Remove from the End

### Method: `removeTail()`

```javascript
removeTail() {
  if (!this.tail) return null;

  const removedValue = this.tail.value;

  if (this.length === 1) {
    this.head = null;
    this.tail = null;
  } else {
    this.tail = this.tail.prev;
    this.tail.next = null;
  }

  this.length--;
  return removedValue;
}
```

### Important idea

This is easier than in a singly linked list because the tail already knows its previous node.

That is one of the main advantages of doubly linked lists.

---

## 17) Search for a Value

```javascript
contains(value) {
  let current = this.head;

  while (current) {
    if (current.value === value) {
      return true;
    }
    current = current.next;
  }

  return false;
}
```

### Explanation

Searching still usually starts from the head and moves node by node.

Even though the list is doubly linked, general search is still **O(n)**.

---

## 18) Full Example

```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(value) {
    this.value = value;
    this.next = null;
    this.prev = null;
  }
}

class DoublyLinkedList {
  constructor() {
    this.head = null;
    this.tail = null;
    this.length = 0;
  }

  append(value) {
    const newNode = new Node(value);

    if (!this.head) {
      this.head = newNode;
      this.tail = newNode;
    } else {
      newNode.prev = this.tail;
      this.tail.next = newNode;
      this.tail = newNode;
    }

    this.length++;
  }

  prepend(value) {
    const newNode = new Node(value);

    if (!this.head) {
      this.head = newNode;
      this.tail = newNode;
    } else {
      newNode.next = this.head;
      this.head.prev = newNode;
      this.head = newNode;
    }

    this.length++;
  }

  removeHead() {
    if (!this.head) return null;

    const removedValue = this.head.value;

    if (this.length === 1) {
      this.head = null;
      this.tail = null;
    } else {
      this.head = this.head.next;
      this.head.prev = null;
    }

    this.length--;
    return removedValue;
  }

  removeTail() {
    if (!this.tail) return null;

    const removedValue = this.tail.value;

    if (this.length === 1) {
      this.head = null;
      this.tail = null;
    } else {
      this.tail = this.tail.prev;
      this.tail.next = null;
    }

    this.length--;
    return removedValue;
  }

  contains(value) {
    let current = this.head;

    while (current) {
      if (current.value === value) return true;
      current = current.next;
    }

    return false;
  }

  printForward() {
    let current = this.head;
    let values = [];

    while (current) {
      values.push(current.value);
      current = current.next;
    }

    console.log(values.join(" <-> "));
  }

  printBackward() {
    let current = this.tail;
    let values = [];

    while (current) {
      values.push(current.value);
      current = current.prev;
    }

    console.log(values.join(" <-> "));
  }
}

const list = new DoublyLinkedList();

list.append(10);
list.append(20);
list.append(30);

list.printForward();   // 10 <-> 20 <-> 30
list.printBackward();  // 30 <-> 20 <-> 10

list.prepend(5);
list.printForward();   // 5 <-> 10 <-> 20 <-> 30

console.log(list.contains(20)); // true
console.log(list.contains(99)); // false

console.log(list.removeHead()); // 5
console.log(list.removeTail()); // 30

list.printForward();   // 10 <-> 20
list.printBackward();  // 20 <-> 10
```

