## 19) Time Complexity

| Operation             | Time Complexity |
| --------------------- | --------------- |
| Add at beginning      | O(1)            |
| Add at end            | O(1)            |
| Remove from beginning | O(1)            |
| Remove from end       | O(1)            |
| Search                | O(n)            |
| Access by index       | O(n)            |

### Why remove from end is O(1)

Because the tail has a direct `prev` reference.

That avoids searching for the node before the tail.

---

## 20) Strengths of Doubly Linked Lists

Doubly linked lists are useful when:

* you need to move in both directions
* you want easier deletion from the end
* you want to remove a node more easily when you already have a reference to it
* you are modeling history, navigation, or undo-redo behavior

---

## 21) Weaknesses of Doubly Linked Lists

Doubly linked lists have some disadvantages:

* each node uses more memory
* code is more complex than singly linked lists
* more pointer updates are required
* bugs are easier to make because both `next` and `prev` must stay correct

---

## 22) Important Interview Insight

A doubly linked list is often better than a singly linked list when:

* backward traversal matters
* deleting from the tail should be efficient
* node removal should be easier once the node is known

But it costs more memory.

So the trade-off is:

**More flexibility, more memory, more complexity**

---

## 23) Common Mistakes

### Mistake 1

Forgetting to update both `next` and `prev`

### Mistake 2

Updating one direction but not the other

### Mistake 3

Forgetting to reset `head.prev` to `null`

### Mistake 4

Forgetting to reset `tail.next` to `null`

### Mistake 5

Not handling the single-node list case

These mistakes can break the structure of the list.

---

## 24) Real-World Examples

Doubly linked lists can be used in:

* browser back and forward history
* music playlist previous/next navigation
* undo and redo systems
* image gallery navigation
* some cache implementations

